    {
      "id": "sp6davt",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. EXCHANGE RATE DETERMINATION (DEMAND–SUPPLY THEORY)"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "In a flexible exchange rate system, currency value is determined by the market forces of demand and supply of foreign exchange.",
        "An increase in demand for foreign currency causes the Rupee to depreciate, whereas an increase in supply leads to Rupee appreciation.",
        "India practices a managed floating exchange rate system, with foreign exchange reserves maintained by the Reserve Bank of India (RBI).",
        "The Balance of Payments (BoP) comprises Current and Capital Accounts, where a Current Account Deficit (CAD) occurs when imports exceed exports.",
        "The USA is India's major export partner, while China is the top import source, and crude oil constitutes India's largest import item."
      ],
      "id": "3eiluty"
    },
    {
